<h2>4. Authentication, User Roles &amp; Permissions Audit</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Defined Roles:</strong> <code>UserRole</code> enum defines strictly three values: <code>CITIZEN</code>, <code>GOVERNMENT_OFFICIAL</code>, and <code>ADMIN</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Contractor Role Status:</strong> There is <strong>no CONTRACTOR enum value</strong> in <code>UserRole</code>. No contractor user can be registered, authenticated, or assigned permissions under the current schema.</li>
  <li><strong>Demo Accounts:</strong> <code>prisma/seed.ts</code> creates reproducible development accounts:
    <ul>
      <li><code>citizen1@example.test</code> &rarr; <code>UserRole.CITIZEN</code></li>
      <li><code>official1@example.test</code> &rarr; <code>UserRole.GOVERNMENT_OFFICIAL</code> (PWD Department)</li>
      <li><code>admin1@example.test</code> &rarr; <code>UserRole.ADMIN</code></li>
      <li><code>suspended1@example.test</code> &rarr; <code>AccountStatus.SUSPENDED</code> (tests rejection)</li>
      <li><code>pending1@example.test</code> &rarr; <code>AccountStatus.PENDING_VERIFICATION</code></li>
    </ul>
  </li>
  <li><strong>IDOR Protection:</strong> Verified in <code>complaints.controller.ts</code>. Citizens querying private complaints must match <code>citizenId === req.user.id</code>. Public tracking at <code>/track</code> uses tracking IDs and strictly redacts all PII.</li>
  <li><strong>Separation of Duties:</strong> Requesting officials cannot approve their own SLA extensions (enforced in <code>sla.controller.ts</code>). Issuing moderators cannot review appeals against their own decisions (enforced in <code>moderation.controller.ts</code>).</li>
</ul>

<h2>5. Citizen Complaint Workflow Audit</h2>
<p>The citizen complaint workflow operates as a complete, auditable state machine backed by interactive database transactions:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Lodge Grievance:</strong> Citizen completes form on <code>/report</code>. Input is validated by Zod. Category existence is verified. A Crockford collision-resistant tracking ID (<code>CIV-YYYY-XXXXX</code>) is generated atomically with initial <code>ComplaintStatusHistory</code> (<code>SUBMITTED</code>).</li>
  <li><strong>SLA Attachment:</strong> The <code>SlaEngine</code> selects a 5-tier policy based on category and priority, computing immutable original vs. current response and resolution deadlines.</li>
  <li><strong>Department Assignment:</strong> Reviewers accept the complaint (<code>UNDER_REVIEW</code> &rarr; <code>ASSIGNED</code>) and assign a department.</li>
  <li><strong>Progress Logging:</strong> Field crews log structured updates (advancing status to <code>IN_PROGRESS</code>) and site inspections.</li>
  <li><strong>Resolution Submission:</strong> Official submits resolution notes, transitioning status to <code>RESOLVED</code> and triggering an in-app verification alert to the citizen.</li>
  <li><strong>Citizen Verification / Reopen:</strong> The reporting citizen reviews completion evidence on <code>/track</code>. Confirming marks the complaint <code>VERIFIED</code>. Rejecting with mandatory explanation transitions it to <code>REOPENED</code> for rework.</li>
</ol>

<h2>6. Official &amp; Department Workflow Audit</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Department Scoping:</strong> Officials are constrained to complaints assigned to their department (<code>assignedDepartmentId === req.user.departmentId</code>).</li>
  <li><strong>Multi-Modal Action Center:</strong> In <code>OfficialWorkspacePage.tsx</code>, officials can record progress updates, log formal site inspections, upload evidence photos, send direct messages, and submit resolutions.</li>
  <li><strong>Evidence Review Queue:</strong> Administrators review uploaded files, inspect cryptographic SHA-256 hashes, approve valid proofs, or quarantine abusive files.</li>
</ul>

<h2>7. Transparency, Maps, Engagement &amp; Public Projects Audit</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Real-Time Transparency:</strong> Metrics, 30-day intake/resolution trends, and department scorecards on <code>/transparency</code> query live database tables without mock values.</li>
  <li><strong>Civic Map:</strong> Interactive Leaflet map with custom pins and ~110m coordinate generalization to protect residential privacy.</li>
  <li><strong>Civic Proposals &amp; Polls:</strong> Full community proposal lifecycle with 1-click citizen endorsements and concurrency-safe poll voting backed by a database unique constraint <code>(pollId, userId)</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Formal Civic Projects:</strong> Tracks municipal capital projects (<code>CivicWork</code>) separate from routine complaints. Displays budget, contractor name, physical progress %, and milestones.</li>
</ul>

<h2>8. Dedicated Contractor Module Audit</h2>
<p>A granular investigation was performed across all 17 capabilities requested:</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>#</th>
      <th>Contractor Capability</th>
      <th>Status</th>
      <th>Discovered Code / Schema Evidence</th>
      <th>Gaps &amp; Missing Implementation</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td>1</td>
      <td>Available Eligible Tenders</td>
      <td><span class="badge-missing">Missing</span></td>
      <td>Enum: <code>CivicWorkStatus.TENDERING</code></td>
      <td>No <code>Tender</code> model, no tender publication API, no UI.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td>2</td>
      <td>Tender Details</td>
      <td><span class="badge-missing">Missing</span></td>
      <td>None</td>
      <td>No tender specifications, BOQ, or deadline models.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
